# Antikode Lead Radar Skill — v1.3.0

Generates a structured lead intelligence report for Antikode BD pitches, as a
clean Markdown file. Always follow all steps in order. Never skip the intake form.

---

## Changelog — v1.3.0

Changed from v1.2.0:
- **Markdown output only** — report now exports as a single portable `.md` file. No HTML, no React, no Babel, no CDN, no build step. Opens in any editor, Obsidian, Lark Doc, GitHub, or pastes straight into a deck.
- **Styling removed** — design token map, theme/accent/font/card-style options, score-ring colors, and the "quick re-style" flow are gone. Markdown carries no styling, so none is collected or applied.
- **Intake form: 1 screen, 4 questions** — Lead Brief only (Q1–Q4 including Lead Count). No styling screen.
- **All research depth retained from v1.2.0** — verification flags, client portfolio mapping, competitor loyalty schemes, multi-PIC contacts, promotion schemes, and source hyperlinks all carry over unchanged.

> Carried over from v1.2.0: double-check & verify, 10+ client portfolio entries per lead, 3–5 competitors, 2–4 PICs, 2–3 promos, 4+ sources, every major claim backed by a source URL.

---

## Step 1 — Read the Credential Deck

Before showing any form, check if the user has uploaded a PDF in the project or conversation.

- If a PDF is present → read it using the pdf skill or bash to extract text
- Extract: services offered, past clients, proof points, project types, notable case studies
- Store these internally — you will use them to personalize each lead's `outreachStrategy` and `valueProp`
- If no PDF is present → ask: *"Do you have a credential or pitch deck to upload? It helps personalize the outreach strategies. You can skip this and continue without it."*
- If no PDF → default proof points: HokBen, Maybank, AXIS, Paragon Corp, Tzu Chi Hospital

---

## Step 2 — Show the Intake Form (1 screen only)

Present ONE screen of questions using `AskUserQuestion`. Show all 4 questions in a single call — no second screen, no styling screen.

### Screen 1 — Lead Brief (4 questions via AskUserQuestion)

```
Q1: Geography (single_select)
Options: Indonesia, Singapore, Malaysia, Southeast Asia (all), Other

Q2: Project types to pitch (multi_select)
Options: New loyalty app (from scratch), Loyalty program revamp,
         Gamification / campaign layer, Web & digital experience redesign

Q3: Company size (single_select)
Options: Enterprise (50+ outlets), Mid-market (10–50 outlets), Both

Q4: How many leads? (single_select)
Options: 5 leads (Recommended), 3 leads, 8 leads, 10 leads
```

> **PIC depth defaults to "Manager to VP level" — do not ask the user.**

After Screen 1 is submitted, confirm the brief back to the user in one line before starting research.

---

## Step 3 — Research Leads

After the user submits the form, run web searches to find real, named companies. For each lead, collect ALL of the following:

### 3a — Company Basics
- `company`, `website`, `industry`, `size` (outlet/branch count)
- `score` (1–10), `scoreReason` (1 sentence), `fit` (2–3 sentences)
- `verified` (true/false — based on whether key data was confirmed by a live source)

### 3b — Client / Outlet Portfolio
Search for the company's known client list (if B2B) or outlet locations / sub-brands (if B2C). Map **10+ entries minimum**, each with:
```
{ name: "Client or outlet name", loyaltyStatus: "No Loyalty" | "Lacking Loyalty" | "Full-scale Loyalty", provider: "optional — loyalty provider name if known" }
```
Loyalty status definitions:
- **No Loyalty** — no known loyalty program whatsoever
- **Lacking Loyalty** — has basic/outdated program (stamp card, simple points) with no engagement mechanics
- **Full-scale Loyalty** — mature app or program with CRM, tiers, gamification; note the provider

### 3c — Competitor Landscape
Find 3–5 direct competitors. For each:
```
{ name, loyaltyStatus, scheme: "description of their loyalty approach", weakness: "where they fall short" }
```
This gives BD a benchmark and an opening for the pitch ("your competitor X already has Y — here's how to stay ahead").

### 3d — Decision Maker PICs
Find 2–4 real contacts at the target company, ranging from Manager to VP level. For each PIC:
```
{
  name: "Full name if findable, or role-based title if not public",
  title: "Job title",
  gender: "M" | "F",
  linkedinUrl: "https://www.linkedin.com/search/results/people/?keywords=[TITLE]+[COMPANY]",
  approachAngle: "1 sentence on how to open the conversation with this specific person"
}
```
- If real names are publicly findable (via LinkedIn search, company site, press releases) — use them
- If not publicly available — use role-based names (e.g. "Marketing Manager (Naavagreen)") and flag that the LinkedIn URL is a search link
- Always include the gender icon (♀/♂) in the rendered output

### 3e — Current Promotion Schemes
Find 2–3 active or recent promotions the company is running. For each:
```
{ type: "Promo name", description: "What it is and why it signals a gap", source: "https://..." }
```
These become conversation openers — e.g. "We noticed you're running a payday promo — here's how a gamification layer would make that 3x more effective."

### 3f — Sources
For every lead, collect a `sources` array of all URLs used:
```
sources: [{ label: "Short label", url: "https://..." }]
```
Minimum 4 sources per lead. Every major data point (outlet count, loyalty status, news event, PIC name) must have at least one corresponding source URL.

### 3g — Scoring

Base score 1–10, then apply modifiers:

| Condition | Score range |
|---|---|
| No loyalty app + enterprise scale + expansion signal | 9–10 |
| Has basic program but clear gap + good size fit | 7–8 |
| Has program, less urgent but strategic fit | 5–6 |

**Modifiers:**
- **+1** if company's own clients/outlets have No Loyalty status (amplifies buying power signal)
- **+1** if competitors have weak/no loyalty schemes (less switching friction)
- **+1** if currently running generic promos only (signals they need a proper loyalty platform)
- **−1** if they already have a full-scale mature gamification layer (lower urgency)

---

## Step 4 — Generate the Markdown Output

Output a single `.md` file saved to `leads/antikode-leads-[geography]-[YYYY-MM].md` (create the `leads/` folder if it does not exist). The file is plain Markdown — no HTML, no inline styling, no scripts.

Sort leads by score, highest first. Use this exact structure:

````markdown
# Antikode · Lead Radar — [Geography] BD Intelligence Report

**Brief:** [Project type(s)] · [Size tier] · Manager to VP level · [Month Year]
**Tags:** [Geography] · [Project type] · [Size] · Manager–VP PICs
**Generated:** [YYYY-MM-DD]

## Summary

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Total leads | [n] |
| High priority (8–10) | [n] |
| Medium priority (5–7) | [n] |
| Average score | [x.x] |

**Top 2 to contact first:** [Company A] ([score]), [Company B] ([score])

---

## 1. [Company Name] — Score: [X]/10 · [✓ Verified / ⚠ Unverified]

**Website:** [url] · **Industry:** [industry] · **Size:** [size]
**Why this score:** [scoreReason]

### ◎ Score Rationale
[fit — 2-3 sentences]

### 🏢 Client / Outlet Portfolio
No Loyalty: [n] · Lacking Loyalty: [n] · Full-scale Loyalty: [n]

| Name | Loyalty Status | Provider |
|---|---|---|
| [name] | [status] | [provider or —] |
| ... (10+ rows) | | |

### ⚔ Competitor Landscape & Loyalty Schemes

| Competitor | Loyalty Status | Scheme | Weakness |
|---|---|---|---|
| [name] | [status] | [scheme] | [weakness] |
| ... (3-5 rows) | | | |

### 👥 Decision Makers (PICs)
- **[Name]** — [Title] [♂/♀] · [LinkedIn search]([linkedinUrl])
  Approach: [approachAngle]
- ... (2-4 PICs)

### 🎯 Current Promotion Schemes
- **[type]** — [description] · [source]([url])
- ... (2-3 promos)

### ◈ Value Proposition for Antikode
[valueProp]

### ◉ Outreach Strategy
[outreachStrategy]

### ✦ Conversation Starters
> "[starter 1]"

> "[starter 2]"

### 🔗 Sources
- [label]([url])
- ... (4+ sources)

---

## 2. [Next Company] ...
````

### Rules for the Markdown output:
- All 9 lead sections appear in the order above, for every lead.
- Loyalty status counters in 3b must add up to the number of client rows.
- Every PIC LinkedIn link and every promo source uses real Markdown link syntax `[text](url)`.
- Conversation starters are blockquotes, one per line.
- Separate each lead with a `---` rule.
- No HTML tags anywhere. No `<div>`, no `<style>`, no emoji-only decoration that breaks plain-text readers.

---

## Step 5 — Present the File

Tell the user the file path, then give a 3-line summary:
- How many leads generated, score range
- Top 2 leads recommended to contact first (with scores)
- One reminder: "The report is plain Markdown — paste it into Lark Doc, Obsidian, or a deck as-is."

---

## Trigger Phrases

This skill activates on any of:
- "start the lead form"
- "generate leads"
- "run lead radar"
- "find leads for antikode"
- "lead research"
- "show me the form"
- "open the lead form"
- "new lead research"

On any of these triggers: go directly to Step 1. Do not ask for clarification first.

---

## Notes

- Always use real, named companies — never placeholder names
- Always web search before writing lead content — do not rely on training data alone
- The credential PDF is the source of Antikode's proof points — if no PDF, use Antikode's known clients (HokBen, Maybank, AXIS, Paragon Corp, Tzu Chi Hospital) as reference
- PIC photos are not scrapeable from LinkedIn — use the gender icon (♀/♂) plus a LinkedIn search URL instead
- Flag outlet counts that fall outside the selected size tier with a note rather than excluding the lead
- Verify every named company exists before including it — do not hallucinate company names
- Output is always a single `.md` file — no HTML, no styling, no build step
